  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM EST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\n- Turning colder; rain showers Wednesday\n\nSkies are clear and temperatures are well in the 60s...over 10\ndegrees above normal. A cold front moved across the cwa earlier\ntoday and highs tomorrow will be below normal in the mid 40s. High\nclouds will begin to filter in tonight ahead of the next clipper.\n\n- Lakeshore rain/snow showers Thursday night\n\nWe're moving into a pattern that will have a taste of winter with\nit. A clipper currently over the intermountain west will take a\ntrack toward northern IN by Wednesday afternoon. This clipper will\nbe the start of a huge upper low development. Light rain showers\nare expected Wednesday afternoon as the surface low arrives, but\nqpf looks limited to around a tenth of an inch. Meanwhile, as the\nclipper moves east, strong north flow will develop behind it and\ndraw another short wave south from Canada. The main dynamics will\nremain over the lake and so we'll have highest PoPs near the lake\nshore Thursday and Friday. Lake effect rain and snow showers are\ngoing to develop as h8 temps fall to -6c, creating lake delta t in\nthe mid teens. If any snow falls, it's likely to be over the\nnorthwest cwa Thursday night. Air temperatures Thursday and Friday\nwill be too warm for snow. However, the air may be cold enough for\nsome light snow Thursday night near the lake shore south of LDM. A\nfew tenths of an inch of snow are possible. However, there's a 50%\nchance of an inch or two and a 30% chance of around 3, generally\nbetween Hart and Ludington. \n\nThe upper low is progd to shear out by Saturday leading to a dry\nweekend. Temperatures will remain chilly through the weekend with\nhighs in the 40s. A slight moderation in temperatures occur early\nnext week when highs approach 50 as ridging moves overhead.",
